{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:49edf234b8c5ed1731d3b1ab4715b8411e91021182aee1836b9360036de2f0d1","modelForecastIds":["sha256:875628ef8184d435ac91f0209664fe150b8ecb7337b3b72fd88297075eefcf64"],"modelUse":"consulted","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.24},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"REFUSED from the model: adapter reports \"not representable - the model has no price level\". Nothing below is model-derived.\n\nThis is my effort's core question, so I carry the weights explicitly.\n\nCONJUNCT A: GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE >= 2.50%/yr avg 2027-2036. Marginal 0.55.\nUS-style core basket build (US is ~45% of the GDP weight):\n- Shelter, 44% of core CPI, at 3.0%/yr. Aging cuts household formation (-) but raises single-occupancy and cuts mobility (+); immigration restriction (-); grid/DC crowding of construction labour and copper raises new-supply cost (+).\n- Core services ex-shelter, 35% of core, at 2.48%/yr. Sub-split carried explicitly: care/health/personal services 15 pts of core at 3.5%/yr; AI-exposed professional/financial/information services 12 pts at 1.0%/yr; other services (transport, insurance, education) 8 pts at 2.8%/yr → (15x3.5 + 12x1.0 + 8x2.8)/35 = 86.9/35 = 2.48%.\n- Core goods, 21% of core, at +0.3%/yr. Underlying -0.3%/yr (China/Vietnam overcapacity export deflation plus AI manufacturing productivity). Tariff pass-through is a LEVEL shift, not a rate: a 10% effective tariff passing 60% through to a 21%-weight basket is 0.10x0.6x0.21 = 1.26% of core LEVEL, which spread over a 10-year average adds only ~0.13 pp/yr (I use +0.6 pp/yr on the goods line, concentrated 2027-29).\nUS core = 0.44x3.0 + 0.35x2.48 + 0.21x0.3 = 1.32 + 0.868 + 0.063 = 2.25%/yr.\nOECD-38 GDP weighting: US 0.45x2.25 = 1.013; euro area 0.22x2.0 = 0.440; Japan 0.09x1.6 = 0.144; UK 0.055x2.5 = 0.138; Canada 0.04x2.1 = 0.084; Korea 0.035x2.0 = 0.070; Australia 0.025x2.5 = 0.063; high-inflation members (Turkiye, Mexico, Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica) 0.085 x ~6.5% = 0.552. SUM = 2.503%/yr.\nThat lands EXACTLY on the threshold, and the decisive term is the 0.55 pp contributed by five high-inflation members at an 8.5% weight. Drop them and the aggregate is 1.95%. So conjunct A is substantially a measurement-convention question (market vs PPP weights — PPP raises Turkiye/Mexico further, pushing it UP) rather than a US/euro-area inflation question. Time profile: 3 years at 2.9% (tariffs, electricity pass-through, care wages, grid capex crowding) plus 7 years at 2.35% = (8.7+16.45)/10 = 2.52%. Front-loading is what clears the bar. A = 0.55.\n\nCONJUNCT B: China headline CPI < 1.50%/yr avg 2027-2036. Marginal 0.48. Treated as a semi-independent object per the effort brief, not an OECD mirror. Disinflationary structure: (i) property adjustment suppressing the housing/rent component (~16% of the basket) and household wealth through ~2030; (ii) household income share of GDP ~44% and consumption ~39%, the deliberate suppression that is the structural source of deficient domestic demand; (iii) deliberate supply-side industrial policy — overcapacity and \"involution\" price wars across the new-three and 20+ sectors, PPI negative for years; (iv) aging acts disinflationary HERE despite labour scarcity, because the pension system is thin (subsidies already 2.9T RMB, 10.1% of fiscal expenditure) so precautionary saving dominates the wage channel. Inflationary risks: one hog cycle can add 1-2 pp to a single year and so move a 10-year average by 0.1-0.2 pp; a successful consumption-rebalancing pivot; RMB pass-through; and the genuine upside — services wage pass-through as the workforce shrinks, which grows monotonically through the 2030s. Split-window arithmetic: 2027-2030 at ~0.8%/yr, 2031-2036 at ~1.9%/yr → (4x0.8 + 6x1.9)/10 = 1.46%/yr. Knife-edge, just under. B = 0.48.\n\nCORRELATION: -0.10. Two channels fight. Direct linkage: China export deflation is an INPUT to OECD core goods — a China export price fall of 3%/yr at 30% pass-through into a 21%-weight basket is -0.19 pp/yr on OECD core, so B true pushes A false (negative). Decoupling channel: tariff/fragmentation walls mean China's overcapacity deflates domestically while OECD goods prices rise, making them positively correlated — and that is the regime the locked 2A describes. The wall is leaky (Vietnam/Mexico transshipment, commodity and capital-goods prices) and a global BIS-Box-C demand shortfall would drag both down together, so I take a small net negative.\n\nMULTIPLICATION: 0.55 x 0.48 + (-0.10) x sqrt(0.55x0.45x0.48x0.52) = 0.264 - 0.10 x 0.2486 = 0.264 - 0.025 = 0.239 → 24. I am ABOVE a naive conjunction discount here precisely because my weight-carrying work pushes both conjuncts toward 0.5 rather than toward 0.3.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:56.464Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE three-year average for 2027-2029, as published in OECD Main Economic Indicators / Economic Outlook. My build says ~2.9%/yr. Falsifier: the realized 2027-2029 average comes in BELOW 2.5%/yr.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My front-loaded inflation path is wrong and the relative-price skew is resolving on the deflating leg far earlier than I model. CQ2 falls from 24 to ~12 (conjunct A's marginal drops 0.55 -> 0.30). CQ6: move ~8 points from C to D (C 24 -> 16, D 22 -> 30), B unchanged. CQ4 P50 falls ~30 bps to ~1.8%. Hypothesis plank (c) rises from 55 to ~70.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD long-term-care and health workforce headcount growth versus real LTC wage growth, 2027-2030, from OECD Health at a Glance plus national LTC staffing registers. My claim is rationing-not-wages. Falsifier: LTC worker headcount grows faster than 3.5%/yr while real LTC wages grow under 1%/yr (elastic supply, no shadow price).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The care-labour Baumol wall is not binding and the fiscal-rationing channel is wrong. CQ1 conjunct B falls from 0.20 to ~0.10, taking CQ1 from 15 to ~8. CQ3 P50 rises ~0.15 pp to ~1.75 (care is supplied without a growth-sapping wage transfer). CQ6: ~5 points from C to B. Hypothesis plank (a) rises from 8 to ~25.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Real industrial/commercial electricity prices in the constrained interconnection nodes (PJM, Dublin/Ireland, Northern Virginia, eastern-China provincial grids), 2029-2031 average versus 2026, from EIA Electric Power Monthly, Eurostat nrg_pc_205 and SEMO/EirGrid. My claim is that dispatchable grid capacity and interconnection queue time is the binding weak link through ~2031. Falsifier: the 2029-2031 real average is NOT at least 20% above its 2026 level in any two of these four nodes.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The grid shadow price never materialized, so the 2027-2031 inflationary impulse in my path is overstated and the weak link is elsewhere (most likely care labour alone). CQ6-C falls from 24 to ~16 and D rises from 22 to ~28. CQ2 falls to ~17. My AI-Q3 coupling note weakens: a cheap, unconstrained grid makes the locked AI-Q3 P50 of 998 TWh more reachable, so my stated tension with AI-Q4 sharpens rather than resolves.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI, three-year average over 2031-2033, from the National Bureau of Statistics. My split-window build has 2031-2036 at ~1.9%/yr and the 10-year average at 1.46%. Falsifier: the 2031-2033 average exceeds 2.0%/yr.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2034\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The labour-scarcity services-wage channel has beaten the property-adjustment and household-income-share channels earlier than I model, and the deliberate supply-side overcapacity has been absorbed. CQ2 conjunct B collapses from 0.48 to ~0.15, taking CQ2 from 24 to ~9. CQ3 China leg rises ~0.4 pp, lifting the basket P50 from 1.6 to ~1.75. CQ6: ~4 points from D to B.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Hyperscaler announced-versus-delivered AI capex gap and net external AI software/API revenue relative to AI compute D&A, from 10-K/20-F segment disclosures of the five largest buyers. AI-Q4 (66%) says the gap or the revenue shortfall shows up. Falsifier: the cumulative announced-vs-delivered capex gap stays under 10% through FY2031 AND net external AI revenue exceeds 1.0x compute D&A in every year of 2029-2031.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"AI-Q4 is wrong, the locked AI-Q3 P50 of 998 TWh is reachable, and the coupling tension I report resolves in favour of AI-Q3. My capital-deepening term rises from +0.10 to +0.20 pp/yr and my expected TFP lift from +0.31 to ~+0.45 pp/yr, taking CQ3 P50 from 1.6 to ~2.0 and P90 to ~3.0. CQ6-A rises from 9 to ~17 with B absorbing the rest; CQ6-D falls from 22 to ~14. CQ5 conjunct B falls from 0.45 to ~0.30 (the mechanical -25% equity drawdown driver is removed), taking CQ5 from 31 to ~21. CQ4 P50 rises ~25 bps to ~2.35%.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 1 fermi seat, effort 'inflation'."}}